WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.800
-Was it Nathaniel Thurm?

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.036
Was that --
Was that his name?

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:06.039
The sweaty --
-Nathan Thurm.

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:07.373
-Nathan Thurm.
-Nathan Thurm would be

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:10.410
a great character for right now.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.547
-Except Nathan Thurm
feels shame.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.549
You know what I mean?
Like, Nathan --

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:19.819
I was actually thinking, like,
now, nobody sweats

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:22.822
during interviews because people
just, like, lie

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:27.059
with no sense of repercussion.

00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:28.962
-Yes.
No, that is very true.

00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:35.668
But the approach of Nathan Thurm
was deny, deny...

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:38.705
-Yes.
-...deny.

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:39.873
"No, I didn't.
You think I did?

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:41.174
That's so funny you think that."

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:43.342
"No, we saw you do it."
"You saw me do it?"

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:44.711
"Yeah."
"I know that.

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:45.979
You don't think know that?"

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:49.048
"I saw you do it," you know?
So he would deny.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:53.452
And then, when cornered, reverse
and pretend he always knew.

00:00:53.452 --> 00:00:54.687
Sound familiar?

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:58.425
And then he would lash out
at the interviewer.

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.993
-That's true.
Now it's like a rule,

00:01:00.993 --> 00:01:03.763
like a how-to book
that's being followed.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:05.565
-It is exactly a how-to book.

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:08.901
It's like, Christopher Guest
was doing the news update,

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:11.004
and I'd always say,
"I know that.

00:01:11.004 --> 00:01:13.072
You don't think I know that?
I mean, I was there.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.107
And by the way,
why do I even listen to you?

00:01:15.107 --> 00:01:17.910
'Cause you're so bland and
boring with nothing to offer."

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:24.851
So it's exactly what the
Washington people, types do.

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:28.421
-Where did you -- Where did you
come up with Nathan Thurm,

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:30.523
that typed of character?
-Nathan Thurm was a --

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:34.260
There was a head of makeup
that year.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.598
Marion Siebert.
And Marion was the most

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:42.968
offensive human being you had
ever seen in your life.

00:01:42.968 --> 00:01:45.104
-[ Laughing ]
-You'd sit there --

00:01:45.104 --> 00:01:49.242
She'd look at you,
sit in the chair, she'd dab.

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:51.945
"You like tired."
"Thank you."

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:53.913
-[ Laughs ]
-"None of your features match.

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:55.815
This is tough for me."
[ Grumbles ]

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:58.250
-[ Laughs ]
-Negative energy.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.720
And finally I'd say,
"Marion, gee,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.823
I look a little pale, don't I"?
And she'd say, "I know that.

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:05.892
You don't think I know that?
I'm a makeup artist.

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:08.094
I would know that."
And she always chain-smoked.

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:10.597
So, Billy Crystal
and Christopher Guest

00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:12.164
and Harry and I,

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:14.834
were gonna write a piece
satirizing "60 Minutes."

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.570
And I was gonna play
Nathan Thurm.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.905
We made him -- He was gonna be
the defense lawyer

00:02:19.905 --> 00:02:23.509
who always represents
the bad guy.

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:25.978
I think, in that case,
his name was Ping E. Lee.

00:02:25.978 --> 00:02:28.047
That was the bad guy.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:30.750
-Anyway, I was his lawyer.
I didn't know how to play it.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.285
And Billy Crystal said,
"Why don't you do it

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:34.186
as Marion Siebert.

00:02:34.186 --> 00:02:36.989
You impersonate her behind her
back all the time anyway."

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:38.390
And I said,
"No, but I'll get caught."

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.159
He said,
"Ah, you won't get caught.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:42.828
No one -- They never know,"
he said.

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:45.097
She always got the same brand.
She always chain-smoked.

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:48.334
And I got the same brand
of cigarette she smoked,

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.436
except I put a wire
down the middle of mine,

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:53.139
so as I smoked it, the ash would
never, ever fall off, you know?

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:54.507
-[ Laughing ]

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:57.376
And then I'd go down to the set,
and I'd forget that Marion

00:02:57.376 --> 00:03:01.513
is going to be there, because
she's the head of makeup.

00:03:01.513 --> 00:03:03.215
So she's over there
with her cigarette.

00:03:03.215 --> 00:03:05.885
I'm here with my cigarette.
They go, "Action."

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.253
Harry Shearer's
playing Mike Wallace.

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:10.856
Says, "Mr. Thurm.
Now, I-I-I believe that

00:03:10.856 --> 00:03:12.892
you know that you're
misrepresenting your client."

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:14.760
And I'd say, "I know that.
You don't think I know that?

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.595
I'm a lawyer.
I would know that."

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:18.498
And the director goes,
"Cut. He's sweating."

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:20.166
And Marion says, "I know that.
You don't think I know that?

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:21.600
I'm a makeup artist.
I can see that."

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.635
-[ Laughs ]
-So it was insane.

00:03:23.635 --> 00:03:25.204
-[ Laughing ]
-It was insane.

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:28.241
And she finally found out.
Her assistant got drunk

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:33.078
after the last show and said,
"How stupid are you, Marion?

00:03:33.078 --> 00:03:37.783
Don't you know
you're Nathan Thurm?

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:39.552
Everybody else does!"

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.188
And she confronted me
at the final party.

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:45.858
And I said,
"Marion...you know,

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:50.262
impersonation is the highest
form of compliment."

00:03:50.262 --> 00:03:51.897
And she said, "I know that.
You don't think I know that?

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:55.167
I know that."
-[ Laughing ]

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.270
I was sad about the fact that
I know you and Steve

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.106
are on tour all the time now.
-Yes.

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:02.107
-Worldwide.

00:04:02.107 --> 00:04:04.043
Obviously, this is not a time
to tour,

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:06.345
but you guys are doing
a show together.

00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:08.981
A Hulu show, correct?
-Yes.

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:11.717
"Only Murders in the Building."

00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:13.486
And we play --

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:16.055
It's kind of like
"The Handmaid's Tale,"

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:18.090
except for everything.
-[ Laughs ] Oh, good.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.427
-And it's about two people who
live in an apartment building

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:24.063
in New York,
and they don't know each other.

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:27.066
They kind of see each other
in the elevator occasionally.

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:28.534
And, um...

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:31.637
But you see them going
to their individual apartments

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:34.073
and obsessed
with true-crime stories.

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:36.275
And then, there is a murder
in the building,

00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:38.377
and they want to solve it.

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:41.047
But because
they're old and tired, they say,

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:43.516
"Well, only murders
in the building.

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:45.017
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:46.151
-"If it's a murder
down the street,

00:04:46.151 --> 00:04:48.855
we don't want to solve it."
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:48.855 --> 00:04:51.356
-And you know what?
Steve is going to play

00:04:51.356 --> 00:04:54.426
all the chalk outlines
on the show, which I think --

00:04:54.426 --> 00:04:56.762
You know.
-Because he's so pale.

00:04:56.762 --> 00:05:00.500
-Pale. Listen,
he puts sunscreen on at night.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:01.867
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:04.670
Obviously,
if you start in the fall,

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.038
you'll have to take measures.

00:05:06.038 --> 00:05:08.174
Will you socially distance
on set?

00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:10.843
Will that be hard for you?
Will that be hard for Steve?

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:16.816
-Well, Steve is socially and
emotionally distant for years.

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:20.286
And, no, the banjo helps him,
though to socially distance.

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:22.154
[ Both laugh ]

00:05:22.154 --> 00:05:24.657
No, I think it's a very --
it's a very cagey idea

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:28.160
to have two 70-year-olds
start a new series

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.728
during a pandemic in Manhattan.

00:05:29.728 --> 00:05:32.398
But what could go wrong?

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:34.366
-[ Laughs ]
-I'm excited.

00:05:34.366 --> 00:05:36.869
We'll be conducting
temperature checks.

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:38.904
-Mm-hmm.
-And the old-fashioned way,

00:05:38.904 --> 00:05:40.773
may I add,
because as you know,

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:45.177
and as you've said many times,
Seth, rectums don't lie.

00:05:45.177 --> 00:05:47.246
-[ Laughs ]
It is one of my --

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:50.416
It's one of my go-tos.
-Yeah.

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:51.750
-I want to congratulate you,
Marty.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.118
You're a grandfather.

00:05:53.118 --> 00:05:55.321
Can you tell us anything
about your --

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:56.488
-I am a first --

00:05:56.488 --> 00:05:58.091
-Life as
a Hollywood grandfather?

00:05:58.091 --> 00:06:00.259
-I am a --
Well, yes.

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:01.693
Hollywood grandfather's
are a little different.

00:06:01.693 --> 00:06:04.463
You know, for example,

00:06:04.463 --> 00:06:06.699
I didn't see the baby yesterday
because he had pilates.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:08.834
You know, it's a different --

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:14.040
But it's very exciting
to have -- I don't know --

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.441
the pitter-patter
of little feet around.

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:17.043
It's like
having David Spade over.

00:06:17.043 --> 00:06:20.012
You know?
-[ Laughing ]

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:22.515
-It's my first grandchild.
Theodore Andrew.

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:25.384
-Theodore Andrew.
-Of Oliver and Alyssa short.

00:06:25.384 --> 00:06:27.052
They call me every Sunday,

00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:29.622
according to my iPhone's
missed calls.

00:06:29.622 --> 00:06:33.659
-[ Laughing ]

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:36.996
-Marty, I want to get you
out of here so you have time

00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:41.266
to turn over to CBS
to catch James.

00:06:41.266 --> 00:06:43.102
I love you, Marty.
Thank you for being here.

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:45.671
-I love you, Seth, more than
any human being in the world,

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:48.241
other than some of my kids.
-[ Laughs ]

